# The certification ledger is never auto-merged.
#
# It is an append-only record, so the obvious answer is `merge=union`, which
# keeps both sides' appended lines instead of raising a conflict. That answer
# is wrong here, and silently so. Tested 07aug2026:
#
#     =====
#     Test Run: B      <- two separate runs, fused into ONE stanza,
#     Result: GATE RED
#     Test Run: A         because the ===== delimiters are common lines
#     Result: GATE GREEN  that git deduplicates
#     =====
#
# The merge succeeded with rc 0. No conflict, no warning, and stqa_history's
# check mode would then read that chimera as the last stanza. A silent
# corruption of the record is the one outcome this package exists to prevent.
#
# `-merge` marks the file unmergeable, so git raises a conflict and a human
# decides which runs are real records. That is the correct semantics: two
# honest ledgers cannot be combined by a rule, only by someone who knows which
# runs were certifications and which were debugging.
#
# The structural fix, if multi-branch certification is ever genuinely needed,
# is one file per run rather than one appended file -- different filenames
# never conflict. See internal/DESIGN-roles.md section 10.
qa/test_history.txt -merge
